Rem ==================================================================
Rem lin.shoddy â€” Shoddy standard library: linear algebra
Rem
Rem Everything that asks a QUESTION of a matrix, or takes one apart:
Rem determinants, inverses, linear systems, echelon forms and rank, the
Rem LU, QR and Cholesky factorisations, characteristic polynomials and
Rem eigenvalues, and the vector words that are geometry rather than
Rem arithmetic. Pure throughout.
Rem
Rem THE TYPE LIVES IN matrix.shoddy, NOT HERE. Matrix, Mat, MatGet,
Rem MatMul, Transp, Ident and the rest are that machine's, and this one
Rem includes it and builds on it. A mill that uses lin will normally
Rem include both, because it has to name Matrix to call anything here:
Rem
Rem     Include "matrix.shoddy"
Rem     Include "lin.shoddy"
Rem
Rem That is the arrangement on purpose. matrix owns a data structure and
Rem its arithmetic; lin owns the algorithms over it. Splitting them means
Rem a program that only multiplies matrices does not carry an eigenvalue
Rem solver, and an algorithm added here never disturbs the type.
Rem
Rem Conventions:
Rem
Rem   * INDICES ARE 1-BASED, as everywhere in Shoddy and as in matrix.
Rem   * COEFFICIENT LISTS ARE HIGHEST POWER FIRST, matching eng.shoddy.
Rem     LinCharPoly of a 3x3 returns four numbers beginning with 1.
Rem   * EVERY FACTORISATION RETURNS ONE RECORD, not a tuple and not two
Rem     words. LinLuOf gives LFactor, UFactor, Perm and PSign together
Rem     because P A = L U is only true of all four at once.
Rem   * SOLVERS REFUSE RATHER THAN MISLEAD, following eng. LinSolve
Rem     checks the determinant before eliminating; a singular system has
Rem     either no solution or infinitely many, and returning one number
Rem     from either case is worse than stopping.
Rem
Rem The ceilings, each stated where it bites:
Rem
Rem   * n ~ 8. Every word here is written for clarity over speed and
Rem     rebuilds a whole matrix per elimination step. LinEigVals and
Rem     LinEigSym iterate, so they feel it first.
Rem   * LinEigVals finds the REAL eigenvalues only, by bracketing sign
Rem     changes of the characteristic polynomial. A complex pair has no
Rem     sign change and neither does a repeated root of even
Rem     multiplicity, so both are missed. Length(LinEigVals(m)) is
Rem     therefore meaningful: short of Rows(m) means some were not found.
Rem     LinEigSym is the complete answer for a symmetric matrix, which
Rem     always has a full set of real eigenvalues.
Rem   * Cancellation. LinCharPoly's coefficients span magnitudes that
Rem     subtraction eats, and every word built on it inherits that.
Rem
Rem Not here: matrix construction, indexing, addition, multiplication and
Rem display, which are matrix.shoddy; scalar and complex mathematics,
Rem which is eng.shoddy and is included for its summation, polynomial and
Rem root-finding words.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "matrix.shoddy"
Include "eng.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- types -----------------------------------------------------------
Rem Record fields are positional; if one of these grows, append.

Rem LFactor/UFactor/Perm/PSign, not L/U/P/S: a one-letter accessor is shadowed
Rem by any local of the same letter, and this machine is full of them.
Rem PSign is the determinant of Perm, which is +1 or -1 and is exactly the
Rem factor LinDet would otherwise lose to the row swaps.
Type LinLu
    LFactor As Matrix
    UFactor As Matrix
    Perm  As Matrix
    PSign As Number

Rem QFactor/RFactor for the same reason. Q and R are both single letters
Rem that appear as loop variables three lines further down.
Type LinQr
    QFactor As Matrix
    RFactor As Matrix

Rem Vectors are the COLUMNS of the matrix, so the k-th eigenvalue in
Rem Values pairs with MatCol(Vectors, k).
Type LinEigen
    Values  As List Of Number
    Vectors As Matrix

Rem The Jacobi sweep's accumulator: the matrix being driven to diagonal
Rem form, and the product of the rotations that got it there.
Type LinJac
    Work  As Matrix
    Basis As Matrix

Rem ---- building and shuffling ------------------------------------------
Rem Everything below is written in terms of LinBuild: give it dimensions
Rem and a rule for one cell and it makes the matrix. Shoddy is pure, so
Rem there is no elimination in place - each step is a new matrix, and that
Rem is the cost stated in the banner.

Def LinBuild(rows As Number, cols As Number, f As [ Number Number -- Number ]) As Matrix
    Mat(rows, cols,
        Map(Range(1, rows * cols),
            Fn(k) => Call(f, Fix((k - 1) / cols) + 1, (k - 1) Mod cols + 1)))

Def LinIsSquare(m As Matrix) As Boolean
    Rows(m) = Cols(m)

Def LinSwapRows(m As Matrix, i As Number, j As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m),
             Fn(r, c) => MatGet(m, Ifte(r = i, j, Ifte(r = j, i, r)), c))

Def LinScaleRow(m As Matrix, p As Number, k As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m),
             Fn(r, c) => Ifte(r = p, MatGet(m, r, c) * k, MatGet(m, r, c)))

Def LinScaleCol(m As Matrix, p As Number, k As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m),
             Fn(r, c) => Ifte(c = p, MatGet(m, r, c) * k, MatGet(m, r, c)))

Rem Columns lo through hi inclusive - the half of an augmented matrix that
Rem carries the answer.
Def LinCols(m As Matrix, lo As Number, hi As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), hi - lo + 1, Fn(r, c) => MatGet(m, r, c + lo - 1))

Rem Side by side. The Min and Max look redundant and are not: Ifte
Rem evaluates BOTH arms, so both MatGets must be in range whichever one is
Rem chosen.
Def LinAug(a As Matrix, b As Matrix) As Matrix
    If Rows(a) <> Rows(b) Then
        Error("LINAUG: THE TWO MATRICES HAVE DIFFERENT ROW COUNTS")
    Else
        LinBuild(Rows(a), Cols(a) + Cols(b),
                 Fn(r, c) => Ifte(c <= Cols(a),
                                  MatGet(a, r, Min(c, Cols(a))),
                                  MatGet(b, r, Max(1, c - Cols(a)))))

Rem ---- measures --------------------------------------------------------

Def LinTrace(m As Matrix) As Number
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINTRACE: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        EngSum(Map(Range(1, Rows(m)), Fn(i) => MatGet(m, i, i)))

Def LinMaxAbs(m As Matrix) As Number
    EngMaximum(Map(ToList(Cells(m)), Fn(v) => Abs(v)))

Rem The Frobenius norm - the length of the matrix read as one long vector.
Def LinNorm(m As Matrix) As Number
    Sqr(EngSum(Map(ToList(Cells(m)), Fn(v) => v * v)))

Rem Symmetry to a tolerance, never to the bit. A matrix assembled by
Rem arithmetic is symmetric in intent and not in the last few digits.
Def LinIsSym(m As Matrix, tol As Number) As Boolean
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        False
    Else
        LinMaxAbs(MatSub(m, Transp(m))) <= tol

Def LinVNorm(v As Array Of Number) As Number
    Sqr(Dot(v, v))

Def LinVUnit(v As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    Let n = LinVNorm(v)
    If n = 0 Then
        Error("LINVUNIT: THE ZERO VECTOR HAS NO DIRECTION")
    Else
        VScale(v, 1 / n)

Rem The cross product is three-dimensional and nothing else. A version
Rem that quietly padded to three would be answering a different question.
Def LinCross(u As Array Of Number, v As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    If Length(u) <> 3 Or Length(v) <> 3 Then
        Error("LINCROSS: THE CROSS PRODUCT IS THREE-DIMENSIONAL")
    Else
        ToArray({ Nth(u, 2) * Nth(v, 3) - Nth(u, 3) * Nth(v, 2),
                  Nth(u, 3) * Nth(v, 1) - Nth(u, 1) * Nth(v, 3),
                  Nth(u, 1) * Nth(v, 2) - Nth(u, 2) * Nth(v, 1) })

Rem ---- elimination -----------------------------------------------------
Rem Partial pivoting throughout: the largest available entry in the column
Rem becomes the pivot. Without it a perfectly ordinary matrix divides by a
Rem number that is only small by accident and the answer is noise.

Def LinPivotGo(m As Matrix, col As Number, k As Number, best As Number) As Number
    If k > Rows(m) Then
        best
    Else
        If Abs(MatGet(m, k, col)) > Abs(MatGet(m, best, col)) Then
            LinPivotGo(m, col, k + 1, k)
        Else
            LinPivotGo(m, col, k + 1, best)

Def LinPivot(m As Matrix, col As Number, from As Number) As Number
    LinPivotGo(m, col, from + 1, from)

Rem An If block, not an Ifte: Ifte evaluates both arms, and the arm not
Rem taken here divides by the pivot.
Def LinElimBelowCell(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number,
                     r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If r <= p Then
        MatGet(m, r, c)
    Else
        If MatGet(m, p, col) = 0 Then
            MatGet(m, r, c)
        Else
            MatGet(m, r, c) - MatGet(m, r, col) / MatGet(m, p, col) * MatGet(m, p, c)

Def LinElimBelow(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m), Fn(r, c) => LinElimBelowCell(m, p, col, r, c))

Def LinElimAllCell(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number,
                   r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If r = p Then
        MatGet(m, r, c)
    Else
        If MatGet(m, p, col) = 0 Then
            MatGet(m, r, c)
        Else
            MatGet(m, r, c) - MatGet(m, r, col) / MatGet(m, p, col) * MatGet(m, p, c)

Def LinElimAll(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m), Fn(r, c) => LinElimAllCell(m, p, col, r, c))

Rem Row echelon form: zeros below each pivot, pivots left to right, rows
Rem of zeros at the bottom. Pivot columns are skipped rather than forced,
Rem so this works on any shape and any rank.
Def LinRefGo(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number) As Matrix
    If p > Rows(m) Or col > Cols(m) Then
        m
    Else
        Let piv = LinPivot(m, col, p)
        If Abs(MatGet(m, piv, col)) <= 0.000000000001 Then
            LinRefGo(m, p, col + 1)
        Else
            LinRefGo(LinElimBelow(LinSwapRows(m, p, piv), p, col), p + 1, col + 1)

Def LinRef(m As Matrix) As Matrix
    LinRefGo(m, 1, 1)

Rem Reduced row echelon form: every pivot is 1 and is alone in its column.
Rem This is the one that answers questions - rank, inverse and the
Rem solution of a system all fall out of it.
Def LinRrefGo(m As Matrix, p As Number, col As Number) As Matrix
    If p > Rows(m) Or col > Cols(m) Then
        m
    Else
        Let piv = LinPivot(m, col, p)
        If Abs(MatGet(m, piv, col)) <= 0.000000000001 Then
            LinRrefGo(m, p, col + 1)
        Else
            Let sw = LinSwapRows(m, p, piv)
            Let one = LinScaleRow(sw, p, 1 / MatGet(sw, p, col))
            LinRrefGo(LinElimAll(one, p, col), p + 1, col + 1)

Def LinRref(m As Matrix) As Matrix
    LinRrefGo(m, 1, 1)

Def LinRowIsZero(m As Matrix, r As Number) As Boolean
    EngMaximum(Map(Range(1, Cols(m)), Fn(c) => Abs(MatGet(m, r, c)))) <= 0.000000000001

Def LinRank(m As Matrix) As Number
    Let z = LinRref(m)
    EngSum(Map(Range(1, Rows(z)), Fn(r) => Ifte(LinRowIsZero(z, r), 0, 1)))

Rem ---- determinant, inverse, systems -----------------------------------

Rem Elimination, not cofactor expansion. Cofactors are n! multiplications
Rem and elimination is n^3; at n = 10 that is the difference between an
Rem answer and a hung program.
Def LinDetGo(m As Matrix, p As Number, sign As Number) As Number
    If p > Rows(m) Then
        sign * EngProdOf(Fn(i) => MatGet(m, i, i), 1, Rows(m))
    Else
        Let piv = LinPivot(m, p, p)
        If Abs(MatGet(m, piv, p)) <= 0.000000000000001 Then
            0
        Else
            If piv = p Then
                LinDetGo(LinElimBelow(m, p, p), p + 1, sign)
            Else
                LinDetGo(LinElimBelow(LinSwapRows(m, p, piv), p, p), p + 1, 0 - sign)

Def LinDet(m As Matrix) As Number
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINDET: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        LinDetGo(m, 1, 1)

Def LinMinor(m As Matrix, i As Number, j As Number) As Matrix
    If Rows(m) < 2 Or Cols(m) < 2 Then
        Error("LINMINOR: NOTHING LEFT TO STRIKE OUT")
    Else
        LinBuild(Rows(m) - 1, Cols(m) - 1,
                 Fn(r, c) => MatGet(m, Ifte(r < i, r, r + 1), Ifte(c < j, c, c + 1)))

Def LinCofactor(m As Matrix, i As Number, j As Number) As Number
    Ifte((i + j) Mod 2 = 0, 1, 0 - 1) * LinDet(LinMinor(m, i, j))

Rem The adjugate is the TRANSPOSE of the cofactor matrix, which is why the
Rem indices arrive the other way round here.
Def LinAdj(m As Matrix) As Matrix
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINADJ: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        LinBuild(Rows(m), Cols(m), Fn(r, c) => LinCofactor(m, c, r))

Rem Gauss-Jordan on [A | I]. The right half is the inverse once the left
Rem half is the identity.
Def LinInv(m As Matrix) As Matrix
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LININV: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        If Abs(LinDet(m)) <= 0.000000000001 Then
            Error("LININV: MATRIX IS SINGULAR - IT HAS NO INVERSE")
        Else
            LinCols(LinRref(LinAug(m, Ident(Rows(m)))), Rows(m) + 1, 2 * Rows(m))

Rem Solves A x = b and returns x. The determinant is checked first: a
Rem singular system has either no solution or infinitely many, and the
Rem elimination would hand back one vector from either case without
Rem saying which it was.
Def LinSolve(a As Matrix, b As Array Of Number) As Array Of Number
    If Not LinIsSquare(a) Then
        Error("LINSOLVE: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        If Length(b) <> Rows(a) Then
            Error("LINSOLVE: THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE IS THE WRONG LENGTH")
        Else
            If Abs(LinDet(a)) <= 0.000000000001 Then
                Error("LINSOLVE: MATRIX IS SINGULAR - NO UNIQUE SOLUTION")
            Else
                MatCol(LinCols(LinRref(LinAug(a, Mat(Rows(a), 1, ToList(b)))),
                               Cols(a) + 1, Cols(a) + 1), 1)

Rem Square-and-multiply, so a large exponent costs log n multiplications
Rem rather than n. A negative exponent inverts first, which is the only
Rem reading of a negative matrix power that means anything.
Def LinPowGo(m As Matrix, n As Number, acc As Matrix) As Matrix
    If n <= 0 Then
        acc
    Else
        If n Mod 2 = 1 Then
            LinPowGo(MatMul(m, m), Fix(n / 2), MatMul(acc, m))
        Else
            LinPowGo(MatMul(m, m), Fix(n / 2), acc)

Def LinPow(m As Matrix, n As Number) As Matrix
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINPOW: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        If n < 0 Then
            LinPowGo(LinInv(m), 0 - n, Ident(Rows(m)))
        Else
            LinPowGo(m, n, Ident(Rows(m)))

Rem ---- factorisations --------------------------------------------------

Rem The partial sum both LU and Cholesky are built from: the first `upto`
Rem terms of row i of the left factor against column j of the right.
Def LinDotPart(l As Matrix, u As Matrix, i As Number, j As Number,
               upto As Number) As Number
    If upto < 1 Then
        0
    Else
        EngSum(Map(Range(1, upto), Fn(s) => MatGet(l, i, s) * MatGet(u, s, j)))

Rem The permutation P such that P A can be factored without further row
Rem swaps: the same pivot search LinDet does, applied to an identity.
Def LinPermGo(m As Matrix, perm As Matrix, p As Number) As Matrix
    If p > Rows(m) Then
        perm
    Else
        Let piv = LinPivot(m, p, p)
        LinPermGo(LinElimBelow(LinSwapRows(m, p, piv), p, p),
                  LinSwapRows(perm, p, piv), p + 1)

Def LinLuUpperCell(a As Matrix, acc As LinLu, k As Number,
                   r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If r = k And c >= k Then
        MatGet(a, k, c) - LinDotPart(LFactor(acc), UFactor(acc), k, c, k - 1)
    Else
        MatGet(UFactor(acc), r, c)

Def LinLuLowerCell(a As Matrix, acc As LinLu, u As Matrix, k As Number,
                   r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If c = k And r > k Then
        (MatGet(a, r, k) - LinDotPart(LFactor(acc), u, r, k, k - 1)) / MatGet(u, k, k)
    Else
        MatGet(LFactor(acc), r, c)

Rem Doolittle: L carries a unit diagonal and U carries the pivots. One
Rem column of each per step, and the step never reads what it is writing.
Def LinDooGo(a As Matrix, acc As LinLu, k As Number) As LinLu
    If k > Rows(a) Then
        acc
    Else
        Let n = Rows(a)
        Let u = LinBuild(n, n, Fn(r, c) => LinLuUpperCell(a, acc, k, r, c))
        If MatGet(u, k, k) = 0 Then
            Error("LINLUOF: A ZERO PIVOT SURVIVED PIVOTING - THE MATRIX IS SINGULAR")
        Else
            Let l = LinBuild(n, n, Fn(r, c) => LinLuLowerCell(a, acc, u, k, r, c))
            LinDooGo(a, LinLu(l, u, Perm(acc), PSign(acc)), k + 1)

Rem P A = L U, with all four parts in one record because the identity is
Rem only true of all four together. PSign is the determinant of P, and
Rem LinDet(a) is PSign times the product of UFactor's diagonal.
Def LinLuOf(a As Matrix) As LinLu
    If Not LinIsSquare(a) Then
        Error("LINLUOF: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        If Abs(LinDet(a)) <= 0.000000000001 Then
            Error("LINLUOF: MATRIX IS SINGULAR - IT HAS NO UNIT-DIAGONAL LU FACTORISATION")
        Else
            Rem pm, not perm: a local named perm would shadow the Perm
            Rem field accessor and LinDooGo could not read it back out.
            Let n = Rows(a)
            Let pm = LinPermGo(a, Ident(n), 1)
            LinDooGo(MatMul(pm, a),
                     LinLu(Ident(n), MatFill(n, n, 0), pm, LinDet(pm)), 1)

Def LinColDot(m1 As Matrix, j As Number, m2 As Matrix, k As Number) As Number
    Dot(MatCol(m1, j), MatCol(m2, k))

Rem Column k of A with its projections onto the first k-1 orthonormal
Rem columns of Q taken out - Gram-Schmidt, one column at a time.
Def LinGsCell(a As Matrix, q As Matrix, k As Number,
              r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    Rem One pair of brackets around the whole subtraction: a continuation
    Rem line that begins with - outside brackets is a new statement.
    If c = k Then
        (MatGet(a, r, k)
             - EngSum(Map(Range(1, k - 1),
                          Fn(j) => LinColDot(q, j, a, k) * MatGet(q, r, j))))
    Else
        MatGet(q, r, c)

Def LinQrGo(a As Matrix, q As Matrix, k As Number) As Matrix
    If k > Cols(a) Then
        q
    Else
        Let raw = LinBuild(Rows(a), Cols(a), Fn(r, c) => LinGsCell(a, q, k, r, c))
        Let len = Sqr(LinColDot(raw, k, raw, k))
        If len <= 0.000000000001 Then
            Error("LINQROF: THE COLUMNS ARE LINEARLY DEPENDENT - NO QR FACTORISATION")
        Else
            LinQrGo(a, LinScaleCol(raw, k, 1 / len), k + 1)

Rem A = Q R, with Q's columns orthonormal and R upper triangular. R is
Rem recovered as Q'A rather than accumulated, which is one matrix multiply
Rem against a page of index arithmetic.
Def LinQrOf(a As Matrix) As LinQr
    If Rows(a) < Cols(a) Then
        Error("LINQROF: NEEDS AT LEAST AS MANY ROWS AS COLUMNS")
    Else
        Let q = LinQrGo(a, MatFill(Rows(a), Cols(a), 0), 1)
        LinQr(q, MatMul(Transp(q), a))

Def LinCholCell(a As Matrix, l As Matrix, j As Number, djj As Number,
                r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If c = j And r = j Then
        djj
    Else
        If c = j And r > j Then
            (MatGet(a, r, j) - LinDotPart(l, Transp(l), r, j, j - 1)) / djj
        Else
            MatGet(l, r, c)

Rem A = L L', for a symmetric positive definite A. The square root under a
Rem negative is exactly where positive definiteness fails, so that is
Rem where the refusal goes: it is a fact about the matrix, not a rounding
Rem accident, and no other test is needed.
Def LinCholGo(a As Matrix, l As Matrix, j As Number) As Matrix
    If j > Rows(a) Then
        l
    Else
        Let d = MatGet(a, j, j) - LinDotPart(l, Transp(l), j, j, j - 1)
        If d <= 0 Then
            Error("LINCHOL: MATRIX IS NOT POSITIVE DEFINITE")
        Else
            LinCholGo(a, LinBuild(Rows(a), Rows(a),
                                  Fn(r, c) => LinCholCell(a, l, j, Sqr(d), r, c)),
                      j + 1)

Def LinChol(a As Matrix) As Matrix
    If Not LinIsSym(a, 0.000000001) Then
        Error("LINCHOL: NEEDS A SYMMETRIC MATRIX")
    Else
        LinCholGo(a, MatFill(Rows(a), Rows(a), 0), 1)

Rem ---- characteristic polynomial and eigenvalues ------------------------

Rem Faddeev-LeVerrier. It gets every coefficient from traces of matrix
Rem products and never expands a determinant symbolically, which is what
Rem makes a characteristic polynomial computable here at all.
Def LinFlGo(a As Matrix, m As Matrix, cs As List Of Number, k As Number) As List Of Number
    If k > Rows(a) Then
        cs
    Else
        Let mk = MatAdd(MatMul(a, m), MatScale(Ident(Rows(a)), Nth(cs, Length(cs))))
        LinFlGo(a, mk, Append(cs, 0 - LinTrace(MatMul(a, mk)) / k), k + 1)

Rem Highest power first, so LinCharPoly of a 3x3 is four numbers starting
Rem with 1 and feeds straight into EngPolyEval, EngPolyDeriv and
Rem EngCubicReal without reversing anything.
Def LinCharPoly(m As Matrix) As List Of Number
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINCHARPOLY: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        LinFlGo(m, MatFill(Rows(m), Rows(m), 0), { 1 }, 1)

Rem Cauchy's bound: every root of the polynomial lies inside this radius.
Rem It is generous, and generous is what a scan wants.
Def LinRootBound(cs As List Of Number) As Number
    1 + EngMaximum(Map(DropN(cs, 1), Fn(c) => Abs(c) / Abs(Nth(cs, 1))))

Def LinScanGo(cs As List Of Number, lo As Number, step As Number,
              k As Number, acc As List Of Number) As List Of Number
    If k <= 0 Then
        acc
    Else
        If EngPolyEval(cs, lo) = 0 Then
            LinScanGo(cs, lo + step, step, k - 1, Append(acc, lo))
        Else
            If EngPolyEval(cs, lo) * EngPolyEval(cs, lo + step) < 0 Then
                LinScanGo(cs, lo + step, step, k - 1,
                          Append(acc, EngZeroOf(Fn(x) => EngPolyEval(cs, x),
                                                lo, lo + step)))
            Else
                LinScanGo(cs, lo + step, step, k - 1, acc)

Rem THE REAL EIGENVALUES, AND ONLY THOSE. The scan finds sign changes of
Rem the characteristic polynomial; a complex pair produces none, and
Rem neither does a repeated root of even multiplicity. So the LENGTH of
Rem the answer carries information: fewer than Rows(m) values means some
Rem eigenvalues are not real, or not simple, and were not found. For a
Rem symmetric matrix the set is always complete - and LinEigSym is faster
Rem and gives the vectors as well.
Def LinEigVals(m As Matrix) As List Of Number
    If Not LinIsSquare(m) Then
        Error("LINEIGVALS: NEEDS A SQUARE MATRIX")
    Else
        Let cs = LinCharPoly(m)
        Let r = LinRootBound(cs)
        LinScanGo(cs, 0 - r, r / 1000, 2000, { })

Rem ---- the symmetric eigenproblem --------------------------------------
Rem Cyclic Jacobi: rotate one off-diagonal pair to zero at a time and let
Rem the sweeps chase the rest down. It is slower than the alternatives and
Rem it always converges for a symmetric matrix, which is the trade this
Rem machine wants.

Def LinRotCell(p As Number, q As Number, cs As Number, sn As Number,
               r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    If r = p And c = p Then
        cs
    Else
        If r = q And c = q Then
            cs
        Else
            If r = p And c = q Then
                sn
            Else
                If r = q And c = p Then
                    0 - sn
                Else
                    Ifte(r = c, 1, 0)

Def LinRot(n As Number, p As Number, q As Number, cs As Number, sn As Number) As Matrix
    LinBuild(n, n, Fn(r, c) => LinRotCell(p, q, cs, sn, r, c))

Def LinJacobiAt(j As LinJac, p As Number, q As Number) As LinJac
    If Abs(MatGet(Work(j), p, q)) <= 0.000000000000001 Then
        j
    Else
        Rem tan(2 theta) = -2 a[p][q] / (a[p][p] - a[q][q]). The minus is
        Rem the whole rotation: with the other sign the sweep turns the
        Rem pair to its LARGEST value instead of to zero, and the diagonal
        Rem it leaves behind is orthogonally similar to A - so the trace
        Rem and the orthonormality still check out - but is not its
        Rem eigenvalues. Only A v = lambda v catches it.
        Let theta = 0.5 * EngAngleOf(MatGet(Work(j), p, p) - MatGet(Work(j), q, q),
                                     0 - 2 * MatGet(Work(j), p, q))
        Let rot = LinRot(Rows(Work(j)), p, q, Cos(theta), Sin(theta))
        LinJac(MatMul(MatMul(Transp(rot), Work(j)), rot), MatMul(Basis(j), rot))

Def LinJacobiRow(j As LinJac, p As Number, q As Number) As LinJac
    If q > Rows(Work(j)) Then
        j
    Else
        LinJacobiRow(LinJacobiAt(j, p, q), p, q + 1)

Def LinJacobiSweep(j As LinJac, p As Number) As LinJac
    If p >= Rows(Work(j)) Then
        j
    Else
        LinJacobiSweep(LinJacobiRow(j, p, p + 1), p + 1)

Def LinOffCell(m As Matrix, r As Number, c As Number) As Number
    Ifte(r = c, 0, Abs(MatGet(m, r, c)))

Def LinOffMass(m As Matrix) As Number
    EngSum(Map(Range(1, Rows(m) * Cols(m)),
               Fn(k) => LinOffCell(m, Fix((k - 1) / Cols(m)) + 1,
                                   (k - 1) Mod Cols(m) + 1)))

Def LinJacobiGo(j As LinJac, k As Number) As LinJac
    If k <= 0 Then
        j
    Else
        If LinOffMass(Work(j)) <= 0.0000000000001 Then
            j
        Else
            LinJacobiGo(LinJacobiSweep(j, 1), k - 1)

Rem Values and Vectors together, and paired by position: the k-th value
Rem belongs to MatCol(Vectors, k). Two words returning them separately
Rem would run the whole iteration twice and invite the caller to sort one
Rem without the other.
Def LinEigSym(m As Matrix) As LinEigen
    If Not LinIsSym(m, 0.000000001) Then
        Error("LINEIGSYM: NEEDS A SYMMETRIC MATRIX")
    Else
        Let j = LinJacobiGo(LinJac(m, Ident(Rows(m))), 60)
        LinEigen(Map(Range(1, Rows(m)), Fn(i) => MatGet(Work(j), i, i)), Basis(j))
